import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BAQ5ZYJQ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Smart sync","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/sync.md","filePath":"guide/sync.md","lastUpdated":1795160929000}'),r={name:"guide/sync.md"};function n(h,e,i,d,c,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="smart-sync" tabindex="-1">Smart sync <a class="header-anchor" href="#smart-sync" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Smart sync&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Before a run on another machine, the project is there. After it, what you asked for is here. In between, you copy nothing by hand, and nothing you care about is lost on either side.</p><ul><li><strong>Only what changed crosses.</strong> Each side keeps a manifest of its files; a push sends the difference, compressed, through the connection errand already holds. Nothing changed: under a second.</li><li><strong>Through every layer.</strong> The same sync reaches a server over ssh, a Slurm node, a container, a Windows VM, a Windows machine. No rsync, no scp, nothing to install over there.</li><li><strong>What a run made is not deleted.</strong> The copy follows the project, but a file or a directory a run wrote over there is never errand&#39;s to remove.</li><li><strong>Results come back as they are written.</strong> A run&#39;s record when it ends, the directories you name after each command, and the files it <a href="./steps#view-a-file-that-evolves">shows as it goes</a> at once.</li><li><strong>Caches go both ways.</strong> Downloaded data and memoized results reach every machine, computed once. → <a href="./caching">Data and results, computed once</a></li></ul><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>on lmo, from a Mac</th><th style="text-align:right;">sync</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2597 files, 407 MB, cold</td><td style="text-align:right;">20.9 s</td></tr><tr><td>the same, nothing changed</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.73 s</td></tr><tr><td>one file changed</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.78 s</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="how-the-project-crosses" tabindex="-1">How the project crosses <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-project-crosses" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the project crosses&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The push is a <strong>sync by manifest</strong>. Each side keeps a manifest of its files ( size, mtime, hash ). The local side sends its own, the agent over there answers with what it lacks, and whole files cross, compressed. The manifest of the copy is kept in the copy ( <code>.errand-copy</code> ). So two hosts that share a file system ( a cluster&#39;s login and compute nodes ) share it too, with nothing to declare. The agent walks the copy when it starts and when it goes, and trusts the manifest in between.</p><p>Every copy goes this way: the push before a run, <code>errand setup</code>, an image built on another machine ( <code>build_on</code> ), the <code>also</code> checkouts, the pulls of results, zones and views. There is one set of rules, and nothing to install: on Windows, errand needs neither MSYS2 nor rsync.</p><p>A changed file crosses whole: a large file that changes a little ( a checkpoint, a dataset appended to ) is sent again in full. Keep such files in a zone ( <code>back</code>, <code>cache</code> ) or out of the project.</p><h2 id="what-gets-pushed" tabindex="-1">What gets pushed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-gets-pushed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What gets pushed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The project, without the obvious. <code>.git</code>, <code>__pycache__</code>, <code>*.pyc</code>, <code>.mypy_cache</code> and <code>.pytest_cache</code> are no part of a copy. <code>errand-outputs</code> ( at any depth ), <code>build</code>, <code>dist</code>, <code>*.so</code>, <code>*.o</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>.venv</code> and <code>*.egg-info</code> are written over there and left alone. So is what the project&#39;s layers build there, by its exact path ( an image with its <code>.new</code>, <code>.prev</code> and <code>.part</code> ).</p><p><code>errand-outputs</code> is excluded both ways on purpose: each side&#39;s records, plans and builds are its own. The only runs you get back are the ones you asked for.</p><h2 id="the-copy-follows-the-project-carefully" tabindex="-1">The copy follows the project, carefully <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-copy-follows-the-project-carefully" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The copy follows the project, carefully&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The copy over there is kept identical to the project here, within what errand wrote:</p><ul><li><strong>A file the project no longer has</strong> is deleted over there, and errand prints what went.</li><li><strong>A file changed over there</strong>, outside a zone, is reported, with when it was first seen, before it is overwritten; a file a run wrote that the project does not have is moved aside, not removed.</li><li><strong>A directory a run made</strong> ( a build, a collected catalogue ) is not errand&#39;s: a push says it once, with what it holds and how to zone it, then leaves it. It is removed only once it is empty.</li><li><strong>A directory tagged as a cache</strong> ( a <a href="https://bford.info/cachedir/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>CACHEDIR.TAG</code></a>, as pytest, cargo, ruff and loom write ) is left alone on both sides, without being declared.</li></ul><h2 id="zones" tabindex="-1">Zones <a class="header-anchor" href="#zones" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Zones&quot;">​</a></h2><p>What a run writes into the copy, outside <code>errand-outputs</code>, is declared once as a <strong>zone</strong>:</p><div class="language-python vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">python</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">errand.configure( </span><span style="--shiki-light:#E36209;--shiki-dark:#FFAB70;">zones</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> =</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> { </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;/results&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;back&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;build-*&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;build&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;/data-cache&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;cache&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> } )</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>zone</th><th>over there</th><th>after each command</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>back</code></td><td>written by the run, never pushed into</td><td>brought back here ( anchored path only )</td></tr><tr><td><code>build</code></td><td>built there, never pushed into, never deleted</td><td>stays there</td></tr><tr><td><code>cache</code></td><td>added to by both sides, nothing replaced</td><td>what this side lacks comes back ( anchored path only )</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A name ( <code>results</code> ) matches anywhere in the tree; a leading <code>/</code> anchors it at the root. A runner whose tool writes somewhere of its own says so the same way: <a href="/errand/guide/runners/#what-a-tool-writes-elsewhere">What a tool writes elsewhere</a>.</p><h3 id="a-cache-zone" tabindex="-1">A cache zone <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-cache-zone" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A cache zone&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Downloaded data and results too costly to compute again belong to neither side. A <strong>cache</strong> zone is added to on both sides, and nothing in it is ever replaced or deleted:</p><ul><li>the push sends what the copy lacks: a node with no internet finds the data downloaded here;</li><li>what a run downloads or writes there stays, and is no change outside the zones;</li><li>after each command, what the copy has and this side lacks comes back ( a path from the root only, as for <code>back</code> ), so the next machine gets it too;</li><li>a file with other contents on each side is a <strong>conflict</strong>: said at each sync, left as it is on both. Name files after what they hold ( a hash, a version, the parameters ) and there is none;</li><li>a deletion is never carried: a file removed on one side comes back from the other. Remove it on both. errand never prunes a cache zone.</li></ul><p>Every project has one already, <code>errand-cache</code>, where <a href="./caching#a-file-at-a-url"><code>errand.download</code></a> and <a href="./caching#a-result-computed-once"><code>@errand.memo</code></a> keep what they hold.</p><h2 id="what-comes-back" tabindex="-1">What comes back <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-comes-back" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What comes back&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The run&#39;s record</strong>, when it has written its <code>result.yaml</code>, which it writes last: a run still under way stays over there rather than coming back half written. → <a href="./output">Results and records</a></li><li><strong><code>--pull DIR</code></strong> and anchored <code>back</code> zones, after each command. → <a href="./output#bringing-back-more-pull">Bringing back more</a></li><li><strong>Views</strong>, as soon as a run names them: one file at once, or what changed in a directory. → <a href="./steps#view-a-file-that-evolves">view</a></li><li><strong>Cache zones</strong>: what this side lacks.</li></ul><h3 id="results-are-pulled-not-pushed" tabindex="-1">Results are pulled, not pushed <a class="header-anchor" href="#results-are-pulled-not-pushed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Results are pulled, not pushed&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A finished remote run stays over there until something collects it. A command you wait for collects its results as its last step. <a href="./detached#close-the-laptop"><code>errand attach</code></a> collects those of a command whose connection was lost, and <code>errand status</code> or <code>errand watch</code> those of a <a href="./detached"><code>--batch</code></a> submission.</p><p>A pull never deletes here: a run you have that is gone over there stays. Nor does a push delete over there the records of runs made there: a run tree a <code>--out</code> pattern put in the project ( <code>runs/…</code> ) is left alone by the push for every run it holds that is not here, and the push says so.</p><h2 id="next" tabindex="-1">Next <a class="header-anchor" href="#next" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Next&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="./caching">Data and results, computed once</a> · <a href="./output">Results and records</a> · <a href="./layers/ssh">Ssh</a></p>',30)])])}const g=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
